{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test's failure path is causally upstream of the subject's accepted form — for example, when a mutation is installed under a shown-to-fail-first ritual and the failing installation becomes part of the subject's own guard structure.","counterconditions":["If the same mutation survives under a describe-only installation where the test is run after the subject is fixed, the constrain interpretation fails.","If the 1/9 loss is explained by a simple coverage gap unrelated to installation order, the ritual mechanism is irrelevant.","If ordinarily-guarded modules lose 4/4 to 6/7 under the same shown-to-fail-first ritual, the specialness of compile.py disappears.","If a registry-size or author-specific confound explains the distribution, the constraint claim is weakened."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9"},{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc"}],"mechanism":"Shown-to-fail-first installation converts the test from an external measurement into an internal precondition: the subject is modified to expect and reject the failure mode before it is accepted. Under this installation ritual, the test no longer merely reports on a pre-existing object; it selects which object variant can exist. The 1/9 mutation lost in compile.py reflects a subject whose guard was keyed to declared data rather than added behaviour, so the test constrained only the declared surface.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.82},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it only if the test's own failure mode is planted in the subject before the test is first run — the shown-to-fail-first installation makes the test part of the subject's construction, not its observation.","counterconditions":["If the mutation was lost because the guard checked declared data only, the constraint is about guard design rather than installation ritual.","If the same author and week produce different loss rates under a describe-only workflow, the shown-to-fail-first mechanism is not necessary.","If the 3-of-26 distribution is uniform across modules, the compile.py special case is noise.","If a confound such as one repository or arbitrary registry size explains the difference, the claim collapses."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"002779d3ed9dd0a6"},{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12"}],"mechanism":"The planted failure acts as a ritual gate: the subject must first exhibit the failure and then be modified to suppress it, which means the test's expectation is built into the subject's acceptance criteria. Compile.py's 1/9 mutation loss indicates the planted failure was in the code but not in the recorded acceptance surface, so the constraint was partial and left the added-behaviour path unguarded.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test's guard fires on the author's own mutation — if a process guard never fires on its author, it is probably a description, not a constraint.","counterconditions":["If the author's own mutations are always caught by other modules' guards at 4/4 to 6/7, the reflexive condition is not sufficient.","If the 1/9 loss is due to a single oversight rather than a systematic guard design, the constraint interpretation overstates the mechanism.","If the distribution is explained by module size or registry size rather than guard reflexivity, the claim is confounded.","If a shown-to-fail-first installation with a non-reflexive guard still loses mutations, the condition is not necessary."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"03be16ddea3ab3e2"},{"block":"028950f9751ab59a"}],"mechanism":"The constraint relation is reflexive: a guard that only catches others' mutations describes an imagined subject, while a guard that catches the author's own mutation constrains the actual construction. The compile.py 1/9 mutation loss shows a guard that checked declared data and therefore missed added behaviour by the same author, so the test described a narrower subject than the one installed.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.68},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test's failure mode becomes a required precondition for the subject's acceptance — the shown-to-fail-first installation makes the failure part of the subject's identity, so the test cannot describe what the subject would have been without it.","counterconditions":["If the same mutation loss occurs under a describe-only installation, the shown-to-fail-first precondition is not doing the work.","If ordinarily-guarded modules show the same loss distribution, the installation ritual is not the differentiator.","If the confound of one author and one week explains the distribution, the claim is weakened.","If the 1/9 loss is an artefact of the mutation proposal process rather than the installation, the constraint mechanism is misattributed."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9"},{"block":"002779d3ed9dd0a6"}],"mechanism":"Under shown-to-fail-first, the subject is not accepted until it has failed the test and been repaired. The repair is part of the subject's construction, so the test is a constraint on the repair process. The 3-of-26 result and the 1/9 compile.py loss reflect that only mutations aligned with the planted failure mode were constrained; mutations outside that mode were described but not constrained.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"need_context"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the subject's acceptance is conditional on the test's failure being impossible — the shown-to-fail-first installation turns the test into a sufficiency condition, so the subject is only what survives the test.","counterconditions":["If the test is not a necessary condition for acceptance, the sufficiency claim is too strong.","If the mutation loss is due to an independent coverage gap, the constraint interpretation is not warranted.","If the distribution is explained by registry size or module complexity, the sufficiency condition is confounded.","If the same author's other modules lose 4/4 to 6/7 under the same ritual, the sufficiency condition is not stable."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12"},{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc"}],"mechanism":"The constraint relation is modal: the test sets a boundary that the subject cannot cross after installation. The 1/9 mutation lost in compile.py under shown-to-fail-first installation indicates that the boundary was set too narrowly — the guard covered declared data but not added behaviour, so the test constrained only a subset of the subject. The 3-of-26 distribution suggests the boundary is uneven across modules.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.41},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test's guard is installed as part of the subject's own registry — if the guard lives in the same registry as the subject, the test and subject are mutually constraining, and the distinction between describing and constraining collapses.","counterconditions":["If the guard is outside the subject's registry but the mutation is still lost, the collapse is not necessary.","If the distribution is explained by one author and one week rather than registry structure, the mechanism is confounded.","If the same registry produces different loss rates across modules, the registry is not the determining factor.","If the shown-to-fail-first installation is removed and the loss rate changes, the registry is not sufficient."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9"},{"block":"03093dbd40142ed5"}],"mechanism":"The registry is the point of collapse: when the guard and the subject share a registry, the test's failure mode is part of the subject's accepted vocabulary. The 1/9 mutation lost in compile.py suggests that the guard was keyed to the registry's declared data, so mutations that added behaviour outside the registry were not constrained. The 3-of-26 result reflects the registry size and the arbitrariness of what the registry includes.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"stuck"},"typicality":0.23}],"simulation_proposals":[]}